  {
   "id": "snow-2026-08-07",
   "date": "2026-08-07",
   "title": "SNOW +3.72R / +3.54R \u2014 the 40-day hold, in our own words",
   "source": "debrief answered by Gabriel and Aldemar on exit day, before the outcome could rewrite it",
   "trade_ids": [
    "tr_import_gabriel_020",
    "tr_1785692318200_3831"
   ],
   "honest_flags": [
    "The exit reason (Friday, weekend risk, 'it's been long enough') is a TIME-AND-COMFORT reason, not a levels reason. It worked out; it is not repeatable logic.",
    "The breakeven-not-trail discovery contradicts the coded 2.5xATR trail policy AND the written exit-at-2R policy. Three different behaviours now exist: what's coded, what was written, what actually won.",
    "Checking frequency rose sharply in the final days \u2014 attention followed the urge to act. Worth watching whether more checking causes more selling."
   ],
   "policy_resolution": "Q13 answered: trail from 2R. The written 'full exit at +2R' rule is retired. New sequence: breakeven ~+1R -> hold -> from +2R trail to structure -> exit into strength at OI resistance or on regime break.",
   "rules_going_forward": [
    "Stop to breakeven around +1R, then LEAVE IT ALONE. Do not trail mechanically below +2R \u2014 the SNOW bounce-off-breakeven is the recorded evidence for why.",
    "Trail only from +2R (their answer to Q13), and trail to structure \u2014 a gamma flip or OI support \u2014 not to a formula.",
    "When the urge to sell hits, the first move is RAISE THE STOP, not exit. Fear becomes protection.",
    "Before any discretionary exit, check: SNOW-style checklist \u2014 GEX regime and levels, industry performance, SPX regime, price action. If all four still support the trade, the urge is not a reason (their answer to Q12).",
    "A Friday/weekend exit is allowed but must be NAMED as comfort, not dressed as analysis."
   ],
   "what_actually_happened": [
    "Stop stayed at BREAKEVEN from roughly +1R until the day before exit \u2014 not a trail. One day price came within cents of breakeven and bounced; a mechanical trail would have been hit. The 'loose' stop is why the trade survived to +3.7R.",
    "First serious urge to sell arrived only on day ~39, triggered by a red premarket gap \u2014 not by boredom, not by the gain itself before that.",
    "The response to that urge was to RAISE THE STOP (to 294, above the 293 gamma flip), not to sell. The fear got converted into protection instead of an exit.",
    "What made holding possible: the GEX read (support levels that cushion drops), and neither wanting to be the one who sold while the other held.",
    "There was no plan to sell at 320. The exit was 'profits after a long time, and it's Friday before an unknowable weekend' \u2014 by our own admission more relief than analysis.",
    "Deep positive gamma was doing real work: without the briefing we maybe still sell, but the regime was the reason we were comfortable at all."
   ]
  }
 ],
